   "date": "2015-05-19T17:56:43Z",
   "subject": "Improving occupancy model performance",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi,</div><div><br></div><div>I am building an occupancy model in Stan and am wondering if I am missing any tricks to improve performance. I read through chapter 20 of the manual, but nothing jumped out at me. I&#39;ve based my model off of examples provided on the group forum that marginalizes out the discrete latent variables (<a href=\"https://groups.google.com/forum/#!searchin/stan-users/occupancy/stan-users/EpX-wzOj7L4/v6Dgg-QkX9MJ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/forum/%23!searchin/stan-users/occupancy/stan-users/EpX-wzOj7L4/v6Dgg-QkX9MJ&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792464665188000&amp;usg=AOvVaw08o482ng5tZsiUCLvQOYls\">here</a> and <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/forum/#!searchin/stan-users/occupancy/stan-users/ZuLmNl4Q9xc/OhYbS5V2f6EJ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/forum/%23!searchin/stan-users/occupancy/stan-users/ZuLmNl4Q9xc/OhYbS5V2f6EJ&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792464665188000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1NQ8_1ZejUf3QWshK3_Ln1\">here</a>, thank you Quresh and Bob!).</div><div><br></div><div>I am building up to a more complicated model and right now have 3 models. Each builds upon the previous model and is slightly more complicated than the previous model:</div><div><br></div><div>Model 1: This model includes a probability of occurrence (psi) and a probability of detection (P). I used the logit functions for stability (and parameters muP and muPsi). I also used a binomial function to vectorize the bernoulli function in the example previously posted and avoid a for loop over the Y matrix (also the real data I eventual plan on analyzing is in this format).</div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">muPsi </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  alpha<br>muP    </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> gamma</span></div></code></div><div><br></div><div>Model 2: This model includes a trend, tau, through time acting upon the occurrence.  <br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">muPsi</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  alpha </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> tau </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>muP    </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> gamma </span></div></code></div><div><br></div></div><div>In the the code, I avoid a for loop by using vectors<br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">muPsi </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  alpha </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> tau </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> yearLessOne</span></div></code></div>where</div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">yearLessOne </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">nYears </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div></div><div><br></div><div>Model 3: includes model 2 plus a moving average for muPsi</div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">muPsi </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  alpha </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> tau </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> yearLessOne </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> epsilon</span></div></code></div></div><div>where </div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">  epsilon</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigmaEta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">year </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">nYears</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    epsilon</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">year</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rho </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> epsilon</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">year </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">],</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigmaEta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span></div></code></div><div><br></div><div>In this model, rho is not recoverable. Usually all or almost all of the epsilon parameter include 0 in their 80% CrI (see example output). I suspect this why Stan does not recover rho. Does this seem like a reasonable explanation?  <br></div></div><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><a href=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-IAyic2hDtuU/VVtxFZJIKGI/AAAAAAAAATw/jZRUJFbCMkc/s1600/example.jpeg\" style=\"margin-left:1em;margin-right:1em\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-IAyic2hDtuU/VVtxFZJIKGI/AAAAAAAAATw/jZRUJFbCMkc/s1600/example.jpeg&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792464665189000&amp;usg=AOvVaw127Qp0dNGBy9A7ttAHakOj\"><img src=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-IAyic2hDtuU/VVtxFZJIKGI/AAAAAAAAATw/jZRUJFbCMkc/s320/example.jpeg\" border=\"0\" width=\"320\" height=\"320\"></a></p><div><br></div><div>I would like make sure my code is optimized for two reasons. First, I am using model 3 to build more complicated model and am having slower convergence with it. Second, my ultimate goal is use multiple (100 or more) &quot;fake data&quot; sets as described in chapter 8 of Gelman and Hill (2007) and compare the model&#39;s ability to recover parameters across a range of parameter values. <br></div><div><br></div><div>I have attached 4 files (plus the embedded figure). My R script and the 3 models as Stan files. I am using RStan version 2.6.0 on Windows 7 with R version 3.2.0 (although I don&#39;t think that matters). Please let me know if you need anything else.</div><div><br></div><div>Richard Erickson<br></div><div><br></div><div>PS Bob, when I get this working, I would be willing to write up examples for the Stan manual.</div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
